  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 235 AM EST Sunday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry, seasonable weather continues early this week. \nClouds will increase today, but no rain is expected. \n\n~1035mb surface high pressure is centered across the northern Mid-\nAtlantic, and is ridging southward into the area. Temperatures are \nin the 40s with high clouds over the area well in advance of a \napproaching weak shortwave (that is currently over western KY/TN). \n\nThe surface high is progged to gradually shift eastward later today \nthrough Monday, leading to weak onshore flow each day and highs \ngenerally in the upper 60s-around 70F. The warmest temps will tend \nto occur away from the coast due to continuing onshore flow keeping \ncoastal locations a few degrees cooler. The shortwave will cross the \narea on Monday, leading to continued cloudiness. However, rain \nchances remain very low (<5%) due to weak forcing aloft and dry low \nlevels. The surface high moves farther offshore on Tuesday as an \nupper ridge axis moves overhead. The low level flow becomes SE on \nTue, and temps warm well above average with 70s expected area-wide \nalong with much more sunshine than is expected today/Monday. \nInterior portions of southern VA and NE NC could approach the upper \n70s. Warm again on Wednesday with high temps ranging through the 70s \n(80F readings are not out of the question across interior portions \nof SE VA/NE NC).\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A chance for showers returns Thursday and Friday, \nbut confidence remains low. Above average temperatures are also \nexpected to continue through at least the first few days of November.\n\nThe seasonably warm temperatures continue into mid-late week as that \naforementioned ridge shifts a bit to the east (but may be slow to \nmove offshore). A strong low pressure system at the surface and \naloft will initially move from the Rockies into the northern Plains \nand Midwest early in the week, while a trailing cold front \neventually tries to advance toward the area in the Thursday-Friday \ntimeframe. Shower chances return by Thursday into Thursday night as \nthe trailing cold front gets close to the area. However, models \ncontinue to trend downward with respect to precip in our area as the \nbest upper forcing and height falls likely miss us to the north. \nNevertheless, the ensembles show a 30-50% of 0.5\" of rain across the \narea from Thursday-Friday. As the previous forecaster mentioned, \nmost of the ensembles still show a relative minima in rainfall over \nour area when compared to areas to our west, southwest, and north. \nHowever, this still could change and we continue to advertise a \ngeneral 40-50% PoP from Thursday morning into Friday. Highs remain \nat least in the low-mid 70s through the end of the week, with dew \npts potentially rising well into the 60s by late week. There won't \nbe much in the way of appreciable CAA behind the front, and above \naverage temperatures may continue through next weekend. There remain \nno potential freezes in the forecast as the more substantial cold \nair remains in the northern half of Canada for the foreseeable \nfuture.",
